/// Full behavioral sandbox for the gh-1172 direct-push path of
/// scripts/auto_release.sh: a bare origin whose main carries pubspec 0.1.495
/// tagged v0.1.495 [tagAgeHours] ago (3h = past the 2h coalesce window) plus
/// one pending commit, a seed clone the script runs in, and a `git` shim that
/// can advance origin/main from a second clone right before each
/// `git push origin HEAD:main` ([raceMode] `once`/`always` — E1 race
/// injection). No `gh` stub: the direct-push path is git + python3 only.
AutoReleaseRun runAutoReleaseDirect(
  String name, {
  int tagAgeHours = 3,
  bool dryRun = false,
  String raceMode = 'never',
}) {
  final root = Directory(
    '${_fixtureRoot.path}/direct-$name-${DateTime.now().microsecondsSinceEpoch}',
  )..createSync(recursive: true);
  final origin = '${root.path}/origin.git';
  final seed = '${root.path}/seed';
  final racer = '${root.path}/racer';
  final bin = '${root.path}/bin';
  Directory(bin).createSync(recursive: true);

  // auto_release.sh uses GNU `sed -i` (CI-authored, ubuntu); BSD hosts need
  // the empty-suffix form — transparent shim, real sed either way.
  File('$bin/sed').writeAsStringSync(r'''
#!/usr/bin/env bash
if [ "${1:-}" = "-i" ]; then shift
  if /usr/bin/sed --version >/dev/null 2>&1; then exec /usr/bin/sed -i "$@"
  else exec /usr/bin/sed -i '' "$@"; fi
fi
exec /usr/bin/sed "$@"
''');
  Process.runSync('chmod', ['+x', '$bin/sed']);

  final realGit = _resolveRealGit(bin);
  // git shim: forward everything to real git, but in race mode advance
  // origin/main from the racer clone right before each push so the script's
  // FF-only push races for real (the recompute-on-fresh-head path of E1).
  // The countdown lives in a FILE, not an env var: every push spawns a fresh
  // shim process, so an exported decrement would never persist.

The hygiene test already scans composite actions in `.github/actions/**/action.yml`, so a new composite action will be picked up automatically. Good.

Now let's plan the edits.

### Thread 1 fix — read file once in `builtinSkillSourceViolations`

Refactor: extract pure byte-level checker and read once:

```dart
/// Content verdicts for one skill's SKILL.md bytes...
List<String> builtinSkillFileViolations(
  String name,
  List<int> content, {
  int minBytes = builtinSkillMinBytes,
}) {
  final bytes = content.length;
  final text = utf8.decode(content);
  if (text.trim().isEmpty) {
    return ['built-in skill "$name": SKILL.md ships EMPTY ...'];
  }
  if (bytes < minBytes) {
    return ['... below the floor ...'];
  }
  return const [];
}
```

Hmm, but the existing message formats include the full path `prompts/skills/$name/SKILL.md`. The existing test asserts `violations[0]` contains 'create-goal' and 'EMPTY', and js-apps case has its own message. To minimize churn and keep messages identical, I'll keep the path-based messages in the source checker but read once:

```dart
final content = await file.readAsBytes();
final bytes = content.length;
final text = utf8.decode(content);
if (text.trim().isEmpty) {
  violations.add('... EMPTY ...');
} else if (bytes < minBytes) {
  violations.add('... $bytes bytes, below ...');
}
```

That's the minimal change (single read, verdict consistent with inspected bytes). TDD rule says add a test reproducing the issue. The pure-function extraction makes it testable: I can add a test on the byte-level behavior — e.g. whitespace-only file reports EMPTY (previously `bytes == 0` check would report EMPTY only for 0 bytes... wait, original: `bytes == 0 || text.trim().isEmpty` → whitespace-only reports EMPTY too, but byte count disagrees: original `bytes` from lengthSync could differ from text length... The observable behavior for tests is identical except under races. A RED test proving single-read: hard to simulate TOCTOU without injection.

Pragmatic TDD: extract `builtinSkillFileViolations(name, bytes/text)` — actually I can restructure so the source checker delegates:

```dart
List<String> builtinSkillMdContentViolations(String name, List<int> content, {int minBytes})
```

Test it directly: empty → EMPTY verdict containing name; whitespace-only utf8 → EMPTY; under-floor → floor verdict with byte count; full → empty. These tests fail to compile before the change (RED — guard API missing), then pass (GREEN). Plus existing integration tests stay green.

I'll do that. Messages: keep the exact wording used in source checker including the path. But the pure function doesn't know the path prefix... The map-based check `builtinSkillContentViolations` has messages like `built-in skill "${entry.key}": SKILL.md ships EMPTY`. The source check messages use full path. To keep it simple and message-identical, I'll keep messages in the source checker and just change the read:

```dart
